The chemistry node's own instruction stands at the head of this: any work on chemistry begins from the open problems of the field, enumerated from the field's own surveys, before any construction is written. This document is that enumeration, read against what the exact model already constructs. Every claim about the model names the module that holds it.

## What the model is, in chemistry

The model starts from relational quantum mechanics (Rovelli 1996; Di Biagio and Rovelli, "Stable facts, relative facts", 2021), carried in chem/facts.cub. There is no absolute state. A fact is a reading of the world relative to the system that holds it, an observation from a state space to an outcome space, and what the fact leaves open is its fiber. A fact of one system is stable for another when the other's record fixes it, which is what decoherence with an environment produces.

On that ground the modules build chemistry with no real number formed:

- chem/atom.cub: hydrogen exactly. Pauli's algebraic solution through the conserved Runge–Lenz vector gives the bound energies as −1/(2n²) Hartree and each shell's states as orbitals l = 0 … n−1 with 2l+1 projections. The shell counts are proved for every n; the energies are exact rationals in the one measured scale.
- chem/spin.cub: angular momentum coupling exactly. Every Clebsch–Gordan coefficient is a sign and the square root of a rational, by Racah's formula.
- chem/charge.cub: the electric interaction exactly. In loop quantum gravity with matter, a charge sits at the open end of a Faraday line of the spin network and Gauss's law holds at each vertex. The field energy between two opposite unit charges is Thomson's minimum over fluxes obeying Gauss's law, and Kirchhoff (1847) gave that minimum as a ratio of two counts of spanning trees. The Coulomb interaction on a network is a rational number computed by counting; the continuum's 1/r is what the ratio becomes on a fine regular network.
- chem/fold.cub: the fold as a fiber. One carrier serves atoms, residues and secondary-structure elements, so a theorem proved once holds at every scale. A bond graph or contact map cannot tell a carrier from its mirror image, so handedness is data those representations do not hold. Template transport along an alignment is sound: an aligned pair takes the template's value, any other pair stays open, and soundness is a path.

Information is conserved throughout. Nothing in the model is approximated away; what a reading does not fix stays in its fiber, explicitly open, and is never filled by a fitted number.

## The problems, and what each needs from the model

### 1. Chemical entity resolution

Deciding when two records are the same substance is the field's central data problem. Standard InChI was meant to be tautomer-invariant and implements that only partially; IUPAC project #2012-023-2-800 has been open since 2012 to redesign it, and standard InChI's success over the tautomeric part of the Cambridge Structural Database is 26.25% (strict 13.46%), as the chemistry node records.

In the model, sameness is a path, not a string match. Two records are the same substance relative to a situation exactly when a construction identifies them under the interconversions that situation makes feasible. Tautomers are one substance where interconversion is fast relative to the observer and two where it is slow: a stable fact relative to an environment, in chem/facts.cub's terms. What the model needs next: interconversion rates as relative facts, so that identity is computed per situation rather than fixed by an identifier.

### 2. Strong electron correlation

Density functional theory carries most of applied chemistry and fails exactly where electrons are strongly correlated: bond breaking, transition-metal catalysis, magnetic materials (Cohen, Mori-Sánchez and Yang, "Challenges for Density Functional Theory", Chem. Rev. 112:289, 2012). The fix in the field is multireference methods whose cost grows steeply with system size.

The model's electronic structure is exact by construction. Charge interaction is a ratio of spanning-tree counts (chem/charge.cub), angular momentum coupling is exact (chem/spin.cub), and shells are exact (chem/atom.cub). Nothing is a functional fitted to data, so there is no exchange–correlation approximation to fail. What the model needs next: the many-electron state on the network, built from these exact pieces, and its cost derived from its definitions, which decides how far exactness reaches before the fiber is left open.

### 3. Folding: mechanism, dynamics, disorder

Structure prediction from sequence is largely achieved for well-folded proteins; the mechanism by which a chain reaches its fold, its conformational dynamics, misfolding and intrinsically disordered proteins are open ("Protein Dynamics Beyond Structure Prediction", arXiv 2606.08647, 2026; Folding@home, on AlphaFold's advance). Disordered proteins defy the structure–function paradigm altogether.

The model treats a fold as a fact stable for its environment (chem/facts.cub): stable for the solvent and every apparatus that records the chain, so every experiment reports the same fold. A disordered protein is then precisely a chain with no such stable fact: its arrangement stays in the fiber, relative to each partner it binds. This turns disorder from an exception into a case of the same definition. What the model needs next: the energy landscape over arrangements, from the exact interactions, and the flow on it whose basins are folds (chem/fold.cub already derives basins from a flow).

### 4. Crystal structure prediction and polymorphism

Polymorphism, one composition in several crystal structures, occurs in at least half of pharmaceutical compounds, and the polymorph controls solubility, stability and processing (Price, Braun and Reutzel-Edens, Chem. Commun., 2016). Computed crystal energy landscapes contain many more structures than are ever observed, and explaining which appear, through the kinetics of crystallization, is the main challenge (Price, Chem. Soc. Rev., 2014).

The model states this exactly: a polymorph is a stable fact relative to the history that produced it, not a property of the molecule alone. The landscape's unobserved minima are arrangements no recorded history has fixed. What the model needs next: crystallization histories as constructions, so that which polymorph appears is computed from the conditions of its formation.

### 5. Homochirality

Why biology uses one handedness of amino acids and sugars is open. The model already proves that handedness is data the bond graph and the contact map do not hold (chem/fold.cub). What the model needs next: the amplification of a small excess, as a flow on handedness with exact interactions, whose stable fact is the single hand.

### 6. Superconductivity and correlated materials

The electronic structure of high-temperature superconductors across their phase diagrams, and whether their transition temperature can reach room temperature, are open. These are the strongly correlated systems of problem 2 at material scale, and exact spin coupling (chem/spin.cub) is the piece pairing rests on.

### 7. Enzymes

Some enzymes show kinetics faster than diffusion allows, and highly active enzymes for arbitrary reactions cannot yet be designed de novo. Both need the reaction as a construction in a situation: exact interactions, the fold as a stable fact, and the rate as a relative fact.

### 8. The heaviest elements

How spin–orbit coupling and other relativistic effects change the chemistry of the trans-actinides, and whether chemistry ends near element 137, are open. The shell structure is exact in chem/atom.cub for the non-relativistic atom; the relativistic correction is the next construction on the same algebra.

## Where this leaves the work

Each problem above has the same shape: the field fixes as absolute a fact that is relative, or fits a number where an exact construction exists. The model's constructions so far are the base every problem needs: exact atoms, exact spin, exact charge, facts relative to their holders, and folds as stable facts. The next constructions are named under each problem, and each is built from these pieces.

